From ba6b3faea53ced97cb0cd5b8443b835d203027fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Paul Kinlan (fleet:chrome-agent-platform)" <paul.kinlan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:59:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf-review: bounded candidate batching, per-batch contract,
 named truncation (rn0ky)

The nightly lost a 47-candidate perf-review to a completion cut mid-JSON at 50,314 bytes (cut after
candidate 45), and the run reached the operator as "no verdict" - which reads as a model that ignored
the contract, sends nobody looking for an output bound, and hides the 45 rows that were nearly there.
Raising the budget was not the answer: 157 s of a 10-minute budget was used, so the binding constraint
was the size of one completion, not time.

Three pieces, and each is provable on its own:

1. lib/candidate_batching.py (new, pure): plan_batches splits a candidate list deterministically,
   bounded by BOTH count and serialized bytes (one oversized record gets a batch of its own rather
   than being dropped); merge_reports concatenates findings in pre-pass order, unions reviewed_commits
   and REFUSES by name a candidate_id answered twice; classify_unparseable names truncation
   structurally (ends inside a string, or unclosed braces) with the evidence kept.
2. factory: a per-batch engine call loop, opt-in per station via
   `output.max_candidates_per_call`. Each batch gets its own prompt, its own raw output
   (model_output.batch<k>.txt), its own parse + field normalisation + schema validation (the output
   CONTRACT, per chunk) and its own artifacts; the recorded report is the deterministic merge. The
   parse path is now one shared function (parse_station_report) so a batch is held to exactly the
   contract the single call was always held to. presented_candidates.json records every id the run
   put in front of the model across all batches, and lib/findings.read_presented_candidate_ids reads
   it first - so a batched run is judged on all of them, not on the first slice of one prompt.
3. OutputTruncatedError(NoVerdictError): a cut completion is a NAMED station failure carrying the
   batch, the byte count, the brace census and the unaccounted ids, written to failure_reason.txt and
   output_classification.json. Still a plumbing failure, so the bounded repair-retry and line
   continuation are unchanged.

perf-review opts in at 8 candidates per call; every other station is untouched (a test pins that an
undeclared bound stays one call).

Tests: 30 unit tests for the module (including the nightly's exact cut-inside-a-string shape), 5
end-to-end tests driving the real dispatcher with a real pre-pass and a stub engine (3 batches over 8
candidates, per-batch prompts/outputs, merged report in pre-pass order, the accounting population,
opt-in behaviour, named truncation for batch 2/3, prose classified as no-json rather than truncation),
and 382 existing tests re-run green (findings, factory_truth, candidate accounting, report_schema,
perf_review, line_andon, keyless broker, containment adapters).

Release-note: none - the perf-review station now answers large candidate lists in bounded batches and reports a truncated completion as truncation instead of "no verdict".
